# The line coverage this repository CLAIMS, written by hk's `coverage` step
# in fix mode (`hk run fix`) and read by the README badge.
#
# WHY A FILE AND NOT A MEASUREMENT: a badge has to be regenerated whenever
# the README is, and measuring costs a full instrumented test run. This is
# the cached answer, and the gate's job is to keep it honest rather than to
# trust it.
#
# V46 calls this the CEILING, and the name is exact: it is what the badge
# claims, and the gate refuses a measurement BELOW it -- a badge that
# overstates is worse than no badge. It also refuses a measurement more than
# half a point ABOVE it, because a claim that far behind the truth is stale
# rather than wrong, and stale claims are how a number nobody trusts gets
# into a README. The FLOOR is separate and lives in `hk.pkl`: a hard minimum
# that failing is a test-coverage problem, not a bookkeeping one.
#
# key is a git SHA over everything under `src/`, which is every file that can
# move the number -- the Rust sources, and the two data files compiled into
# them with `include_str!`:
#
#   git ls-files -s -- src | git hash-object --stdin
#
# README.md is deliberately NOT in the key. It is regenerated FROM this file,
# so including it would make every badge update invalidate the cache that
# produced the badge -- a loop with no fixed point.
#
# ONE DECIMAL, TRUNCATED rather than rounded. Coverage is platform-dependent,
# so a second decimal is a digit no single machine can reproduce; truncating
# keeps two machines that measure 93.98 and 93.94 on the same side of the
# claim, where rounding would not.
